WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
<v Instructor>So now let's talk about CloudHSM.</v>

2
00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.770
And the best way to start is to talk about KMS.

3
00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:08.970
So when we have KMS, AWS manages the software for encryption

4
00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:10.860
and the encryption keys.

5
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.330
But with CloudHSM,

6
00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:16.230
AWS will only provision us the encryption hardware.

7
00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.750
It's going to be dedicated hardware

8
00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.660
and HSM stands for Hardware Security Module,

9
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.520
hence the name CloudHSM.

10
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.100
And your responsibility is going to manage

11
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.330
your own encryption keys entirely, not AWS.

12
00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.940
The idea is that you are entirely responsible

13
00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.150
for the security, the maintenance,

14
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:40.380
and the backup of these encryption keys, not AWS.

15
00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.140
So the HSM device is going to be tamper resistant.

16
00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.120
It has some kind of compliance,

17
00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.480
and so that means that if anyone on site

18
00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.660
tries to open up your HSM device,

19
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.120
there's going to be a flip and it's going to be seen.

20
00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:58.230
So it supports both symmetric and asymmetric encryption

21
00:00:58.230 --> 00:00:59.063
which is really helpful

22
00:00:59.063 --> 00:01:02.700
if you want to do something called TLS or SSL offloading.

23
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.770
And there's no free tier available.

24
00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:06.810
For you to use CloudHSM,

25
00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.820
you need to use the CloudHSM Client Software.

26
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.400
It's not something that is done through API calls.

27
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.490
And you have some integration with CloudHSM,

28
00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:17.040
for example, with Redshift that supports CloudHSM

29
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.500
for database encryption and key management.

30
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.910
So in case you want to use SSE-C type of encryption

31
00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:25.530
for your S3 objects,

32
00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:29.520
which is when you provide your own encryption key to S3

33
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.010
and then there will be server-side encryption happening,

34
00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.130
then a good way to generate that encryption key

35
00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:36.810
would be to use CloudHSM.

36
00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.790
It's a very common pattern.

37
00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:41.159
Okay, so let's have a look at a diagram.

38
00:01:41.159 --> 00:01:43.500
AWS will manage the hardware for us,

39
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.590
but then we will be provided with a CloudHSM device

40
00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.540
and it's dedicated to us

41
00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.610
and then that's all AWS does for us.

42
00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:54.390
So if we lose the encryption keys, if we lose anything,

43
00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.580
AWS cannot recover the device for us.

44
00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:00.060
It's really our own responsibility, so use with caution.

45
00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.920
And to manage these keys and everything,

46
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.570
you need to have CloudHSM clients

47
00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.880
which will access your CloudHSM device

48
00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:06.930
and you manage your keys.

49
00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:08.490
They will have encrypted connection

50
00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:11.130
between your CloudHSM clients and your CloudHSM device,

51
00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.340
of course, to encrypt and share a secure information.

52
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.350
But it's your responsibility

53
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.930
to manage everything within your device.

54
00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.210
AWS cannot help you once it has provided you

55
00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.920
with the hardware you need.

56
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.810
In terms of IAM permissions, therefore,

57
00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.700
the only thing AWS can do

58
00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.480
in terms of IAM is helping you create, describe,

59
00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.180
and delete your CloudHSM cluster.

60
00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.940
But anything within your CloudHSM cluster,

61
00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.980
it's your own responsibility.

62
00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.920
So using the CloudHSM software, you can manage the keys,

63
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.080
that means create, read, update, delete keys,

64
00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.380
and also manage the users that have access to these keys.

65
00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:48.780
It's very similar to say a database

66
00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.050
where AWS will give you the database itself

67
00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.210
and you have to manage the users within it.

68
00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.700
It's not exactly the case, not exactly similar as CloudHSM,

69
00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:57.960
but you get the idea.

70
00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.350
You have to manage all the security within your device

71
00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.330
and AWS cannot help you with that.

72
00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.220
Okay, another very important thing

73
00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.800
is to set up CloudHSM with high availability.

74
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.740
And for this, you need to create a CloudHSM cluster

75
00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.249
that will be spread across multiple AZ.

76
00:03:13.249 --> 00:03:14.430
It's going to be great to enhance

77
00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:16.320
one more time the availability,

78
00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.510
but also the durability of your data.

79
00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.360
So we have this very classic architecture

80
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.000
where we have two AZ, two CloudHSM devices

81
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.950
that are going to be paired up in a cluster

82
00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:27.570
and your CloudHSM clients.

83
00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.280
And the idea here is that if we lose one AZ,

84
00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.170
well, we'll still have access to the second AZ,

85
00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.650
and our data is safe, highly available, and durable.

86
00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:35.820
But important to see it once

87
00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.800
to know that it's possible to do it,

88
00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.530
going into the exam, it can be very handy.

89
00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:43.680
So for CloudHSM, you have a single-tenant device.

90
00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.770
Whereas for KMS, it's multi-tenant.

91
00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:51.717
For Standard, both KMS and CloudHSM are FIPS 140-2 Level 3.

92
00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.860
In terms of the master keys, you have different kinds.

93
00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:59.310
On KMS, you have the AWS Owned Keys, the AWS Managed Keys,

94
00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.590
as well as the Customer Managed KMS Keys.

95
00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:03.240
Whereas on CloudHSM, you just have one thing.

96
00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:05.760
It's Customer Managed CMK.

97
00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.950
Now, for the key type, well, you have similar ones.

98
00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:10.410
You have symmetric, asymmetric, digital signing,

99
00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:12.870
and CloudHSM gives you also hashing.

100
00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:16.560
For key accessibility, KMS keys are scoped per region,

101
00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.870
although you can do KMS key replication.

102
00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:21.060
And CloudHSM, well, they're deployed and managed

103
00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.290
in specific VPC.

104
00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.150
And they can be shared across different VPC

105
00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.730
by using VPC peering, for example.

106
00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.340
CloudHSM has cryptographic acceleration

107
00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:32.400
for SSL and TLS and Oracle TDE.

108
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.420
In terms of access and authorization, KMS is using IAM,

109
00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.660
whereas CloudHSM is using their own users

110
00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.160
and you have to manage your own permissions

111
00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.610
from within the CloudHSM device itself.

112
00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:45.510
For high availability,

113
00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:47.340
well, this is embedded in the KMS service.

114
00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.820
But for CloudHSM, you need to have multiple HSM device

115
00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.130
over different availability zones.

116
00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:55.320
For audit capability, you have CloudTrail and CloudWatch.

117
00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:56.370
But on top of it,

118
00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:59.370
CloudHSM has multifactor authentication support.

119
00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:01.020
And finally, there's a free tier for KMS

120
00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:04.200
whereas there's no free tier for CloudHSM.

121
00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:05.940
Okay, so that's it for this lecture.

122
00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:08.940
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

